# XSH fsetpos()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Every fpos_t passed to fsetpos() comes from fgetpos() on the same stream,
# as the page requires of applications.

assertion  XSH.fsetpos.001
anchor     functions/fsetpos.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/fsetpos.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       On a stream reading "0123456789", with p0 stored by fgetpos() at
           the start and p3 after 3 reads: after 2 more reads fsetpos(f,
           &p3) returns 0 and fgetc() returns '3'; then fsetpos(f, &p0)
           returns 0 and fgetc() returns '0'.
note       fsetpos() came from ISO C (Issue 4); 2001 is the first POSIX.1
           edition with it.
test       t/001-restores.c

assertion  XSH.fsetpos.002
anchor     functions/fsetpos.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       On a stream reading "0123456789" read to end-of-file (feof()
           non-zero), fsetpos() to a value stored after 3 reads returns 0,
           feof() is then 0 and fgetc() returns '3'.
test       t/002-clears-eof.c

assertion  XSH.fsetpos.003
anchor     functions/fsetpos.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       On a stream reading "0123456789", with p stored after reading '0'
           and '1' and then ungetc('x'), fsetpos(f, &p) returns 0 and the
           next fgetc() returns '2', not 'x'.
test       t/003-undoes-ungetc.c

assertion  XSH.fsetpos.004
anchor     functions/fsetpos.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       On an "r+" stream on "0123456789": fputc('X') (at 0), fgetpos() and
           fsetpos() to that value, then fgetc() returns '1'; fgetc() of '2',
           fgetpos() and fsetpos() to that value, fputc('Y') and fclose()
           leave "X12Y456789".
note       After fsetpos() the next operation on an update stream may be
           input or output.
test       t/004-update-switch.c

assertion  XSH.fsetpos.005
anchor     functions/fsetpos.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
says       On a "w" stream on an existing file given a BUFSIZ array of the
           test's with setvbuf(_IOFBF), with p0 stored at the start and
           "abc" still buffered 2 s after the open (UNRESOLVED if not),
           fsetpos(f, &p0) returns 0, the file then holds "abc", and its
           last data modification and status change times are later than
           they were after the open.
note       A CX rule; the change history does not say which edition added
           it, so "since" is left out. The timestamp resolution is
           implementation-defined; the 2 s wait is a practical cap that
           covers whole-second timestamps.
test       t/005-writes-pending.c

assertion  XSH.fsetpos.006
anchor     functions/fsetpos.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2024
says       On a stream from fmemopen(buf, 64, "w") given a BUFSIZ array of the
           test's with setvbuf(_IOFBF), with p0 stored at the start and "abc"
           still buffered (not yet in buf; UNRESOLVED if not), fsetpos(f,
           &p0) returns 0 and buf then begins "abc".
note       Austin Group defect 1225 (Issue 8) aligned the CX rules with
           fseek().
test       t/006-memory-stream.c

assertion  XSH.fsetpos.007
anchor     functions/fsetpos.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       With errno set to ERANGE, a successful fsetpos() on a file stream
           leaves errno ERANGE.
test       t/007-errno-kept.c

assertion  XSH.fsetpos.008
anchor     functions/fsetpos.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      required
says       On a "w" stream given a BUFSIZ array with setvbuf(_IOFBF), with p0
           stored at the start, "abc" still buffered (UNRESOLVED if not) and
           the descriptor closed with close(), fsetpos(f, &p0) returns a
           non-zero value with errno EBADF.
note       The buffer needed to be flushed, so the ERRORS preamble holds.
           The error indicator is not required: XSH 2.3 leaves the order of
           detection open, so the closed descriptor may be found by an
           lseek() before any write (variant fsetpos.lseek_first), and the
           indicator is set for a read or write error. close() does not
           move the offset, so the descriptor is not a second handle (XSH
           2.5.1). The change history shows only that the error predates
           Issue 6, so "since" is left out.
test       t/008-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.fsetpos.009
anchor     functions/fsetpos.html#ERRORS/EFBIG
class      required
since      2024
says       In a child with RLIMIT_FSIZE 100 and SIGXFSZ blocked, on a "w"
           stream given a 4096-byte array with setvbuf(_IOFBF), with p0 stored
           at the start and 200 bytes buffered, fsetpos(f, &p0) returns a
           non-zero value with errno EFBIG, the error indicator is set, and
           SIGXFSZ is pending (when XSI).
note       Austin Group defect 1669 (Issue 8) made the error base; the
           signal is XSI and checked only there.
test       t/009-efbig.c
